WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:07.173
To understand RAG, let's first get a stronger&nbsp;
understanding of Embeddings and Similarity&nbsp;Search.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:18.470
At its core, an embeddings model transforms&nbsp;a string into a vector of a specific dimension or&nbsp;length that is represented in an embedding space.

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:25.333
Within this space, the closer two vectors are,&nbsp;the more similar the 
corresponding strings are&nbsp;semantically.

00:00:25.333 --> 00:00:30.690
Think of it like a map where words&nbsp;and
phrases are represented as locations.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:37.152
Words&nbsp;with similar meanings cluster together,
reflecting&nbsp;their semantic relationships.

00:00:37.152 --> 00:00:47.167
You may have seen&nbsp;one hot encoded vectors in a more conventional&nbsp;ML model, where the vector encodes discrete data&nbsp;via ones and zeros.

00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:50.511
These types of vectors are&nbsp;called sparse vectors.

00:00:50.511 --> 00:00:58.645
While the embeddings we'll&nbsp;use for RAG, in which all values in the vector&nbsp;contain a floating point number that encodes some&nbsp;meaning,

00:00:58.645 --> 00:01:01.291
are known as density vectors.

00:01:01.291 --> 00:01:11.003
Embeddings&nbsp;are produced by machine learning models akin to&nbsp;how other ml models generate numeric forecasts&nbsp;or sentiment classifications.

00:01:11.003 --> 00:01:20.184
We train them on&nbsp;large amounts of text data, enabling them to&nbsp;grasp the nuances of language and represent&nbsp;them in the embedding space.

00:01:20.184 --> 00:01:27.476
One of the first&nbsp;embeddings models used for these kinds of 
tasks&nbsp;was called Words2Vec.

00:01:27.476 --> 00:01:31.814
Today, there are a plethora&nbsp;of embeddings models to choose from.

00:01:31.814 --> 00:01:39.187
Here are a&nbsp;few prominent ones, along with the dimensionality&nbsp;of their embeddings,
in the table on the slide.

00:01:39.187 --> 00:01:46.181
Ada from OpenAI with a dimensionality of 1,536.

00:01:46.181 --> 00:01:52.567
Embed v3 from Cohere with a dimensionality&nbsp;of 1,024.

00:01:52.567 --> 00:02:00.122
And all-MiniLM from Hugging Face with a&nbsp;dimensionality of 384.

00:02:00.122 --> 00:02:07.740
It's worth mentioning&nbsp;that models with higher dimensional outputs&nbsp;can potentially capture more intricate semantic&nbsp;relationships.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:13.524
However, this benefit comes at the&nbsp;cost of storing these longer vectors.

00:02:13.524 --> 00:02:23.527
We mentioned&nbsp;in the last video that embeddings only make sense&nbsp;in the context of other embeddings they are&nbsp;compared with in the same space.

00:02:23.527 --> 00:02:28.428
So now that&nbsp;we have embeddings, how do we leverage them?

00:02:28.428 --> 00:02:31.410
This&nbsp;is where similarity search comes in.

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:44.292
It allows us to&nbsp;calculate the degree of similarity between two&nbsp;embeddings using mathematical operations like&nbsp;cosine similarity, inner product, or squared L2&nbsp;Norm.

00:02:44.292 --> 00:02:50.432
Cosine similarity measures the angle between&nbsp;vectors,
focusing on their direction.

00:02:50.432 --> 00:02:56.591
This is a&nbsp;popular go-to metric for text similarity,
which&nbsp;we'll be using in this course.

00:02:56.591 --> 00:03:03.678
Inner product&nbsp;considers both direction and magnitude,
providing a more comprehensive measure of&nbsp;similarity.

00:03:03.678 --> 00:03:08.906
Do note that if the vectors being&nbsp;compared are all units of equal length,

00:03:08.906 --> 00:03:13.870
then inner&nbsp;product and cosine similarity are basically the&nbsp;same thing.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:20.505
Squared Euclidean measures the straight&nbsp;
line distance between vectors, focusing on their&nbsp;absolute positions.

00:03:20.505 --> 00:03:30.606
On the right of the slide, there&nbsp;are visual representations of cosine similarity, inner product, or dot product, and Square Euclidean respectively,

00:03:30.606 --> 00:03:33.594
along with their mathematical formulas.

00:03:33.594 --> 00:03:42.781
However, comparing a query vector to every single&nbsp;vector in a database to find similar ones would&nbsp;be inefficient and time-consuming.

00:03:42.781 --> 00:03:49.408
To address&nbsp;this, similarity search utilizes indexing,
which&nbsp;groups similar embeddings together,

00:03:49.408 --> 00:03:56.387
enabling&nbsp;searches to focus on smaller areas
where the&nbsp;most similar embeddings already exist.

00:03:56.387 --> 00:04:04.124
Common&nbsp;indexing techniques include inverted file indices
and hierarchical navigable small worlds.

00:04:04.124 --> 00:04:10.346
We won't&nbsp;cover the specifics but just know that they are both 
ways of narrowing a similarity search

00:04:10.346 --> 00:04:15.357
to&nbsp;only the promising areas before computing the&nbsp;distances.

00:04:15.357 --> 00:04:23.718
As you might expect, storing massive&nbsp;vectors, computing distances between them, and&nbsp;indexing embeddings is a lot of work.

00:04:23.718 --> 00:04:31.314
To provide&nbsp;an easier way for developers to interact with&nbsp;embeddings vector databases have become an&nbsp;important tool.

00:04:31.314 --> 00:04:36.953
These databases handle the&nbsp;complexities of storage, retrieval, and querying,

00:04:36.953 --> 00:04:43.736
so all we need to do is feed our data in the&nbsp;proper format and query the vector database&nbsp;for what we need.

00:04:43.736 --> 00:04:53.705
There are so many popular&nbsp;vector databases today but for this course, we'll&nbsp;be using Chroma, a lightweight and scalable open-source vector database.

00:04:53.705 --> 00:05:00.700
Chroma is a great choice&nbsp;for a variety of use cases, and interacting with&nbsp;it will give you the skills you need

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:04.880
to interact&nbsp;with other vector databases in your AI journey.

